// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// Knowledge the attacker holds only in one execution of a principal may not
// be spent while another execution of that same principal is re-executed.
// Alice has exactly two runs available here and they exclude each other:
//
//   - deliver the honest gb, and Alice's ka is g^ab, so Bob's DEC reduces and
//     n is na -- but the attacker cannot open e and never learns na; or
//   - deliver PUBKEY(nil) as gb, and the attacker computes ka as
//     DH_KEX(ga, nil), opens e and learns na -- but Alice's e is now sealed
//     under a key Bob does not hold, so Bob's DEC does not reduce and n is
//     not na.
//
// ga is guarded, so kb is g^ab in every execution and the attacker can build
// neither g^ab nor ENC(g^ab, na) itself. Adding sessions does not help: na#2
// is a distinct value and the guard pins Bob#2 to Alice#2's own a.
//
// The engine used to report an attack that takes na from the second run and
// e from the first: attacker knowledge is global and monotone, and a
// re-execution that leaves e uninstalled forwards Alice's pristine e whatever
// history the knowledge came from. Forwarding the pristine e is itself a
// claim about what Alice produced, so replaying it verbatim is no escape --
// incompatible_histories_mitm.vp is the counterweight where the attacker
// genuinely authors what Bob receives.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private msg
	generates a
	generates na
	ga = PUBKEY(a)
]

principal Bob[
	generates b
	gb = PUBKEY(b)
]

Alice -> Bob: [ga]
Bob -> Alice: gb

principal Alice[
	ka = DH_KEX(gb, a)
	e = ENC(ka, na)
	generates n_c
	c = AEAD_ENC(na, n_c, msg, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: e, n_c, c

principal Bob[
	kb = DH_KEX(ga, b)
	n = DEC(kb, e)
	p = AEAD_DEC(n, n_c, c, nil)?
	h = HASH(p)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: c
]
